<%# Education, on EDUCATION_PLAN's two-sigma argument. The strongest mission
    page in the app, because the inequality it names has actually been
    measured — so it leads with the finding rather than with features. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    In 1984 an educational psychologist named Benjamin Bloom measured
    something uncomfortable. Students taught one-to-one, and moved on only
    once they'd actually mastered a thing, performed two standard
    deviations above students in an ordinary classroom. The average tutored
    student beat 98% of their conventionally taught peers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The finding held up. It just never scaled, because tutors cost forty to
    a hundred pounds an hour and you cannot pace thirty children
    individually on one timetable. So the wealthy buy those two standard
    deviations retail, and everybody else gets the batch version: same
    pace for everyone, teach to the middle, and a report card recording how
    much you hadn't learned when the term ran out.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The inversion</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    School holds time fixed and lets mastery vary — everyone gets a term,
    and the difference between people comes out as grades. Mastery learning
    turns that inside out: <strong class="text-ink">mastery is fixed and
    time varies</strong>. You don't move past fractions until fractions are
    yours. How long that takes is yours too, and it stops mattering the day
    it's done.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    That's what this section is built to do, for whoever in the house is
    learning anything — a child schooling at home, a teenager stuck on one
    topic, or you, at forty, finally learning the thing you always meant to.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("education") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A map of the subject") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Every subject broken into topics, per learner, with each one's state
        visible: not started, being worked on, or genuinely mastered. The
        starting shape comes from published curriculum standards, and then
        it's yours to bend.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Mastery you have to earn") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A topic doesn't go green because someone clicked done. It moves when
        there's evidence — a retrieval check passed, work produced, a
        conversation that showed it. That's what makes the map worth
        trusting a year later.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Review that beats forgetting") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Things you've learned come back for a check at widening intervals,
        scheduled by an algorithm built on how memory actually decays. Ten
        minutes on the right day beats an hour of re-reading the week before
        an exam.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Taught through what you like") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The tutor knows the learner's hobbies, because this app already
        does. Ratios explained through a recipe or a guitar's fret spacing
        are the same ratios, learned by somebody who is actually paying
        attention.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Never just doing the homework</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A model will happily hand a teenager the finished answer, which is the
    fastest way to build a machine that produces good marks and no learning.
    The teaching tools here are built the other way: they explain at the
    learner's level, ask them to try, and check whether it landed. Getting
    the answer out of them is meant to be harder than getting the
    understanding.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The paperwork, if you need it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Households schooling on their own terms have to produce records —
    portfolios, hours logs, something transcript-shaped — and usually
    reconstruct them in a panic each year from memory. Here they generate
    from what the year already recorded, because the evidence was being
    kept as it happened.
  </p>
</section>
